# Step 22 fix, caught by a real verification test before merging, not assumed safe:
# this threshold is a bare "x >= N" check with NO memory of which coordinate
# "epoch" x is in - and N (now 4500, was 4465 before the v2 coords merge) is
# numerically SMALLER than the OLD pre-cutscene space's natural x range
# (5002-12343+). Without a prerequisite, it would already be satisfied the
# instant the OLD "interactive-cutscene trigger" (x>=12210) fires, before the
# player ever touches the real post-cutscene continuation at all - confirmed by
# a direct test that jumped straight to x=12210 under extend_past_end=True and
# saw the boss trigger "fire" immediately, which is wrong. [requires: Survived
# cutscene] (same mechanism as camera-lock-1's own [requires: DO NOT LET GO])
# fixes this: that box can only be visited for real once x has genuinely reset
# into the new post-cutscene space and climbed back past 472.
#
# Step 27 v2-coords merge: threshold moved 4465 -> 4500 per
# coords_captured_from_foxo_v2.list (Foxo's refined capture). The old separate
# ">= 4503 TODO: name this trigger" box from v1 is REMOVED here - v2 dropped it
# entirely (diffed v1 vs v2 directly: the only other change was this same
# edit), apparently superseded by the boss_hit_counter.py mechanism instead of
# needing its own named checkpoint box. v2's own comment for this box is itself
# truncated mid-sentence in the source file as pushed ("...Instead, watch boss"
# - confirmed via wc/tail, not a read artifact on this end) - preserving the
# fuller existing explanation below instead of replacing it with the cut-off one.
>= 4500 [requires: Survived cutscene] # BOSS TRIGGER

# For boss, disable normal X stagnation checks because camera is locked.
# Checked directly (see AGENTS.md "Step 21"): the only stagnation-like mechanism in
# this codebase is STAGNATION_PENALTY/STAGNATION_FREE_ATTEMPTS, keyed specifically to
# repeated re-entry into an "enemy"-kind box - NOT a general x-position stagnation
# check. The boss trigger above is classified as its own "boss_trigger" kind, not
# "enemy", so this mechanism never applies to it regardless - no code change needed,
# confirmed rather than assumed. Instead, watch boss_hit_counter.py's BossHitDetector.
